Hi Phil,
I am working with a set of questions that allows 2 attempts per question. I have fitted a gpcm model where attempts are considered as categories. Therefore, answer can be 0, 1 and 2. Where 0 = incorrect answer in two attempts, 1 = answered correctly in 2nd attempt, 2 = answered correctly in 1st attempt. Note, the reason I used '1' for 2nd attempt and '2' for first attempt is that I wanted the answer to be ordered by theta.
Once I fit the model with 'gpcm', I am getting the following parameters. (refer to the screenshot pls). My expectation was that items.d0 < items.d1 < items.d2 given that I have ordered my categories (likert scale). That is indeed the case for most of the questions. However, for some questions, this expectation is not met,  items.d2 < items.d1 (pls refer to the yellow highlighted questions).

My question is:
1) How should i interpret the items.d* where items.d2 < items.d1 ?
2) When computing the probability of selecting a category, P(x=k), can you use the absolute value of the items.d instead of considering the negative sign? order of value is preserved if I ignore the negative sign.

regards,
Azman Sami

